## 4. Flexible class creation

### 4.1 Creation form

Retain and improve the existing Classes & Students screen.

Fields:

| Field | Required | Policy |
|---|---|---|
| Academic year | Yes | Server-known year |
| Academic level | Yes | Canonical level |
| Class name | Yes | User-defined |
| Planned capacity | Yes | Existing range 1–60 unless policy changes |
| Room | No | Free text |
| Assigned Teacher | No | Separate operation |
| Initial roster | No | Separate operation |

Supported naming examples:

- `1ère A`
- `1ère B`
- `Classe 1`
- `Les Étoiles`
- `Les Papillons`
- `الأولى أ`
- `قسم الياسمين`

No required letter or number suffix.

The same class name must not be duplicated within the same academic year and level under the existing database uniqueness rule.

### 4.2 Growth during registration

The system must continue allowing additional class creation after registration opens.

Example:

- School prepares `1ère A` and `1ère B`.
- Registration demand increases.
- An authorized user creates `1ère C`.
- The additional class initially has an empty roster.
- Staff subsequently place eligible students into it.

Creating `1ère C` must not automatically transfer students, modify another class's capacity, or assign a Teacher.

### 4.3 Class lifecycle

Recommended states, subject to final data-model review:

- `PLANNED`: created ahead of operation.
- `READY`: reviewed and prepared for normal school use.
- `ACTIVE`: class operating during the school year.
- `ARCHIVED`: retained as historical data.

**Contract caveat:** `school_classes` currently contains `is_archived`, not this complete status vocabulary. These states are a proposed UX/lifecycle requirement, not permission to add database columns immediately.

If existing fields can faithfully support an accepted behavior without inventing lifecycle state, reuse them. Otherwise, define the smallest necessary change in a separately approved Academics migration.

Archiving must not delete students, memberships, Attendance or historical reports.

